@startuml d10_embedded_hybrid_locked
!include _style.puml
title D10 — embedded-hybrid-locked  (D9's tables; a trigger that stays correct under concurrency)

entity "person" as p {
  * person_id : BIGINT <<PK>>
  --
  * charity_id, full_name, email, joined_at
  --
  <back:#E7DDF5>* recent_donations : JSONB — newest 20</back>
}
entity "donation" as d {
  * donation_id : BIGINT <<PK>>
  --
  * person_id, <back:#FFF3CD>charity_id</back>, amount_cents, currency, donated_at, note
}
p ||--o{ d
d -[#2E7D32,thickness=2]-> p : "trigger (fixed)"

note right of p
  **Identical to D9** in schema, indexes,
  queries and write statements.
  Only triggers.sql differs.
end note

note bottom of d
  **D9's trigger corrupted 3-5 donor caches per 30 s**
  under concurrent reads and writes, while passing
  every isolated audit. Two bugs, two fixes:

  |= bug |= symptom |= fix |
  | ordering race | adjacent ids swapped | <back:#D8F0DE>merge + sort by (donated_at, id)</back> instead of prepend |
  | lost update | a committed donation missing | <back:#D8F0DE>lock person FOR UPDATE</back> before the rebuild |

  The lost update is the subtle one. The rebuild's
  UPDATE waits on the row lock; PostgreSQL re-checks
  the target row after the wait but does not re-run the
  subquery against a fresh snapshot, so it writes a
  cache missing the donation that just committed.
  Locking in a separate statement first gives the
  rebuild a snapshot taken after the competitor commits.

  **D9 → D10 prices correctness itself.**
end note
@enduml
